{"id":"new-line-25-million","title":"The line moves from $10 million to $25 million","question":"Now that voters have raised the line for a Board vote, how many of the contracts of recent years would no longer reach one?","finding":"The November 10 preliminary count showed 63.76% yes. On November 9 the City Attorney expected the Charter changes to take effect in mid- to late December after formal filing.. Applied to contracts that began in 2023 to 2025, 312 were reached by the old line and 225 would be reached by the new one: 90 contracts worth $1.39 billion sit between $10 million and $25 million. 33 of the 90 are with nonprofits and 8 are keyed under a rule that sets competition aside. Most are held by Public Utilities Commission (18); City Administrator (16); Airport Commission (11); Public Health (10). The Board’s own analyst, counting the items the Board actually voted on (new contracts and amendments), put it at 304 items falling to 115 and about $2.51 billion no longer reviewed. First-crossing amendments still require approval. After approval under the new rules, further approval is required when cumulative increases since the last approval reach 50 percent of the original amount. This table cannot test amendments.","how":"The rule and its date (3 November 2026) are taken from the project’s case file on the measure and the City’s ballot digest. The new line is applied to the Supplier Contracts table: contracts not typed as construction, grouped by the year the term began, with today’s agreed amount. Term length uses the tenth calendar anniversary of the recorded start date. An end date on that anniversary counts under the new rule. Inclusive-end conventions and missing term dates can change borderline results. Rows list every such contract of $10 million to $25 million that began in 2023 or later, largest first, with the purchasing rule keyed on it. The analyst’s figures are from the trajectory in the case file, which cites the analyst’s report of 8 October 2026.","limits":"Voters approved the change, the old line had not moved since 1988, and the analyst notes $25 million roughly restores its value after inflation. The calculation is retrospective. It does not identify a contract that missed an actual vote. This count and the analyst’s differ by design: the analyst counted files the Board voted on, including amendments to older contracts; this counts contracts by start year at today’s amounts. The classification applies the thresholds to published contract fields. It does not establish an actual vote on any listed agreement. What would settle it: the list of contracts and amendments between $10 million and $25 million signed after the effective date.","tier":"OFFICIAL","columns":[{"k":"payee","t":"With","type":"org"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"contract","t":"Contract","type":"text"},{"k":"number","t":"Contract number","type":"text"},{"k":"start","t":"Term starts","type":"date"},{"k":"end","t":"Term ends","type":"date"},{"k":"agreed","t":"Agreed amount","type":"usd"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid so far","type":"usd"},{"k":"rule","t":"Rule keyed on it","type":"text"},{"k":"nonprofit","t":"Nonprofit","type":"text"},{"k":"signs","t":"Warning signs elsewhere","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Airport Commission","contract":"United Airlines Phase1 T3 West","number":"1000034604","start":"2024-07-01","end":"2025-08-31","agreed":23150000,"paid":12035384,"rule":"other","nonprofit":"","payee":"United Airlines Inc","payeeKey":null,"signs":0},{"department":"Sheriff","contract":"PRETRIAL SERVICES - PIA","number":"1000038760","start":"2026-07-01","end":"2029-06-30","agreed":22532145,"paid":2623983,"rule":"competed","nonprofit":"yes","payee":"San Francisco Pretrial Diversion Project","payeeKey":"SAN FRANCISCO PRETRIAL DIVERSION PROJECT","signs":7},{"department":"Airport Commission","contract":"AIR50379.02 A/N People Movers","number":"1000033160","start":"2024-07-01","end":"2029-06-30","agreed":22100000,"paid"